* ======================================================================================
* SASSI-EDU tutorial example 9: steel-framed building on a reinforced-concrete mat
* ======================================================================================
* What you learn
*   - how a steel-framed building is modelled for SSI: columns, girders, floor beams and braces as
*     BEAMS elements with the section properties of real AISC W and HSS shapes (R table), the K node
*     that puts the strong axis where it belongs (local axis 2 points to K, so the web points to K and
*     I3 is the strong-axis inertia Ix), and the KI/KJ end releases of simple shear connections and
*     pinned column bases;
*   - composite floor slabs as SHELL elements on the beam nodes, a 1.2 m reinforced-concrete mat as
*     SHELL elements whose nodes are the interaction nodes, equipment as lumped masses (MT);
*   - damping by material: SASSI's hysteretic damping is defined per element, so welded steel (2 %),
*     bolted steel (4 %) and concrete (4 %) keep their own values (ASCE 4-16 response level 1);
*   - the SSI frequency shift of a light steel frame on a heavy mat and what it does to the ISRS, the
*     brace forces and the drifts, against the same frame on a practically rigid site (the fixed-base
*     reference of lesson 1);
*   - inter-storey drifts with RELDISP: one run per storey, each referred to the floor below (RELFILE).
*
* Physics in one paragraph
*   A three-storey braced steel frame (621 t with its slabs and equipment) stands on a 21 m x 15 m
*   mat (925 t) on a medium-dense sand site.  On a fixed base it sways in X at 4.38 Hz (85 % of the
*   mass); on the soil the mat slides and rocks, and the system frequency drops by 9 % to 3.98 Hz.
*   The steel has only 2-4 % damping and the radiation damping of rocking is small at this frequency,
*   so the roof transfer-function peak comes down by only 9 %.  This record is stronger at 4.0 Hz than
*   at 4.4 Hz: with SSI the floor spectra and brace forces are as high as or higher than on the fixed
*   base, and the drifts are 16-21 % higher (the braced bays rock on the mat).  For a light structure
*   on a heavy mat, SSI moves the response more than it reduces it.
*
* The structure (x, y = 0 at the centre of the building; z up, grade z = 0)
*   Plan: column lines 1-4 at x = -9, -3, 3, 9 m, lines A-C at y = -6, 0, 6 m (6 m bays)
*
*        y=6   C1 ====XX==== C2 ========== C3 ====XX==== C4
*               I            |            |            I
*               XX           |            |            XX       XX       X-braced bay (two HSS diagonals)
*        y=0   B1 ---------- B2 ---------- B3 ---------- B4     === and I girders with shear-tab connections
*               XX           |            |            XX       --- and | moment-frame girders (welded)
*               I            |            |            I
*        y=-6  A1 ====XX==== A2 ========== A3 ====XX==== A4     floor beams on x = -6, 0, 6 (not drawn)
*             x=-9          -3            3            9
*
*   X direction: X-braced frames on lines A and C (end bays) and a moment frame on line B.
*   Y direction: X-braced frames on lines 1 and 4 (both bays) and moment frames on lines 2 and 3.
*   The middle bays are free of braces (equipment access, pipe routing).
*
*   Elevation of line A (looking north):
*     z = 14.0   +=====+=====+=====+   roof
*                | \ / |     | \ / |
*                |  X  |     |  X  |    storeys 4.5 m
*     z =  9.5   +=====+=====+=====+   floor 2
*                | \ / |     | \ / |
*                |  X  |     |  X  |
*     z =  5.0   +=====+=====+=====+   floor 1
*                | \ / |     | \ / |
*                |  X  |     |  X  |    storey 5 m
*     z =  0.0 ######################### mat
*               A1    A2    A3    A4
*
*   Members (AISC shapes: A992 W shapes, A500 Gr. C HSS):
*     columns      W14x132 (storeys 1-2), W14x90 (storey 3)
*     girders      W24x76 on lines A, B, C, 2 and 3; W21x44 on lines 1 and 4
*     floor beams  W21x44 on x = -6, 0, 6 (3 m apart, 6 m spans)
*     braces       HSS8x8x1/2 (storeys 1-2), HSS6x6x1/2 (storey 3)
*     slabs        composite, 0.15 m, on floors 1 and 2 and the roof
*     mat          1.2 m thick, 21 m x 15 m (1.5 m beyond the column lines), on the ground surface
*   Equipment: two 30 t heat exchangers (floor 1), four 10 t cabinets (floor 2), two 12 t air-handling
*   units (roof).
*
* What to observe (examples/README.md, Example 9):
*   - CHECK: no error and no warning for both models (HINGED lists four nodes, explained below);
*   - every transfer function is 1.000 at 0.1 Hz (within 0.08 %);
*   - fixed base: the roof X transfer function peaks at 20.2 at 4.37 Hz (an eigenvalue analysis of the
*     HOUSE matrices with the mat clamped gives 4.38 Hz, 85 % of the mass); with SSI at 18.3 at 3.98 Hz
*     (-9 %), and the mat rocks (vertical motion of its edges 0.67 per unit control motion);
*   - 5 % ISRS peaks 3-5 % higher with SSI (roof 10.4 g against 10.0 g), 2 % ISRS peaks 24-32 % higher
*     (roof 20.6 g against 15.6 g): the record's 2 % spectrum peaks at 4.07 Hz;
*   - storey-1 braces 934 kN (SSI) against 887 kN (fixed base); the base shear of 5928 kN divided by the
*     8 storey-1 braces and cos theta = 0.768 gives 965 kN (the braces carry 95 % of the shear);
*   - inter-storey drifts 7.9, 7.7 and 7.4 mm with SSI, 6.5, 6.7 and 6.4 mm on the fixed base.
*
* How to run (the working directory must be the examples directory, see examples/README.md):
*     cd examples
*     ../.venv/bin/sassi run ex09_steel_frame.pre
*   or from the repository root:  .venv/bin/sassi --cwd examples run ex09_steel_frame.pre
* Run time about 30 s (two models: SSI and the fixed-base reference); about 60 MB in examples/ex09*.
* Results: examples/ex09/ (SSI), examples/ex09_fixed/ (the same frame on a practically rigid site).
*
* Units: m, kN, t, s.  Gravity 9.81 m/s2.  Weights are specific weights (kN/m3).
* ======================================================================================
MDL,ex09,ex09
TIT,Ex09 - three-storey braced steel frame on a 1.2 m RC mat, vertical SV (X) input
GRAVITY,9.81
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- soil profile
* L,<nm>,<thick>,<weight>,<Vp>,<Vs>,<pdamp>,<sdamp>   (strain-compatible properties)
*   L 1: medium-dense sand, 0-6 m (Vs 250 m/s), in 1 m sublayers:  Vs/(5h) = 50 Hz
*   L 2: dense sand and gravel, 6-18 m (Vs 400 m/s), 1 m sublayers: 80 Hz
*   L 3: very dense gravel, 18-30 m (Vs 600 m/s), 2 m sublayers:    60 Hz
*   L 4: rock half-space (Vs 1500 m/s)
* Every sublayer passes the 30 Hz cut-off of the frequency set (h <= Vs/(5 f_cut), manual 4.1.2 item 12).
L,1,1.0,19.0,500,250,0.04,0.04
L,2,1.0,20.0,800,400,0.03,0.03
L,3,2.0,21.0,1200,600,0.02,0.02
L,4,1.0,23.0,3000,1500,0.01,0.01
* 24 TOPL layers (the manual recommends more than 20): 6 x L1, 12 x L2, 6 x L3
TOPL,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,2,2,2
TOPL,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,3,3
TOPL,3,3,3,3
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- frequencies
* dt 0.005 s, NFFT 8192: df = 0.0244 Hz (frequency numbers n = f / df).  97 SSI frequencies:
*   0.1, 0.5 .. 2.5 every 0.5 Hz and 2.75 Hz;
*   3.0 .. 5.0 Hz every 0.098 Hz (n step 4): the SSI peak (3.98 Hz) and the fixed-base peak
*   (4.38 Hz) of a frame with only 2-4 % damping are sharp (half-power width 2 beta f = 0.26 Hz);
*   5.25 .. 14 Hz every 0.25 Hz (floor vertical modes 7.2-9.8 Hz, second X mode 11.8 Hz);
*   14.5 .. 30 Hz every 0.5 Hz (99.9 % of the X mass is in modes below 20 Hz; 30 Hz = the cut-off,
*   the low end of the manual's 30-40 Hz for soil sites).
FREQ,1,4,20,41,61,82,102,113,123,127,131
FREQ,1,135,139,143,147,151,155,159,163,167,171
FREQ,1,175,179,183,187,191,195,199,203,205,215
FREQ,1,225,236,246,256,266,276,287,297,307,317
FREQ,1,328,338,348,358,369,379,389,399,410,420
FREQ,1,430,440,451,461,471,481,492,502,512,522
FREQ,1,532,543,553,563,573,594,614,635,655,676
FREQ,1,696,717,737,758,778,799,819,840,860,881
FREQ,1,901,922,942,963,983,1004,1024,1044,1065,1085
FREQ,1,1106,1126,1147,1167,1188,1208,1229
* SITE: 20 generated half-space sublayers, half-space = L 4, control point at the free surface (x')
SITE,0,1,0,20,4,1,0,1,4096,1,0,0.005,8192,1
* vertically incident SV wave
WAVE,2,1,1,1,0
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- nodes
* Mat nodes 1-165: a 15 x 11 grid at grade with 1.5 m spacing, x fastest:
*     node = 15 j + i + 1       x = -10.5 + 1.5 i  (i = 0..14),  y = -7.5 + 1.5 j  (j = 0..10)
*   The mesh passes Vs/(5h) = 250/(5 x 1.5) = 33 Hz > 30 Hz (the lambda/5 rule for the interaction
*   nodes).  Column bases: 17 + 60 R + 4 L (L = 0..3 for lines 1-4, R = 0..2 for rows A-C):
*   A1-A4 = 17, 21, 25, 29;  B1-B4 = 77, 81, 85, 89;  C1-C4 = 137, 141, 145, 149.  Mat centre 83.
N,1,-10.5,-7.5,0
N,15,10.5,-7.5,0
FILL,1,15
NGEN,10,15,1,15,1,0,1.5,0
* Floor nodes: a 7 x 5 grid with 3 m spacing on each level k (z = 5, 9.5, 14), numbered bottom up:
*     node = 165 + 35 (k - 1) + 7 j + i + 1      x = -9 + 3 i  (i = 0..6),  y = -6 + 3 j  (j = 0..4)
*   floor 1 (z = 5): 166-200, floor 2 (z = 9.5): 201-235, roof (z = 14): 236-270; floor centres 183,
*   218, 253.  Every node lies on a beam: girders on the column lines, floor beams on x = -6, 0, 6.
N,166,-9,-6,5
N,172,9,-6,5
FILL,166,172
NGEN,4,7,166,172,1,0,3,0
NGEN,2,35,166,200,1,0,0,4.5
* K nodes of the roof beams: a copy of the roof 4.5 m higher (271-304).  A beam's K node only
* orients it; K-only nodes are not drawn and AFWRITE fixes them (CHECK counts them as used).  The
* last copy (x = 9, y = 6) would be no beam's K node: NDEL removes it (it is the highest number, so
* the numbering keeps no gap).
NGEN,1,35,236,270,1,0,0,4.5
NDEL,305
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- materials
* M,<nm>,<E>,<nu>,<weight>,<pdamp>,<sdamp>,<type 1>
*   Damping (hysteretic, per element) from ASCE 4-16 Table 3-2, response level 1 (low stress,
*   consistent with the uncracked concrete of ex08 and the manual's Damping Cutoff note):
*   reinforced concrete 4 %, welded or friction-bolted steel 2 %, bearing-bolted steel 4 %.
*   1: mat, f'c 40 MPa: E = 4700 sqrt(f'c) = 30 GPa, 24 kN/m3
*   2: floor slabs, f'c 28 MPa: E = 25 GPa.  Weight 43 kN/m3 x 0.15 m = 6.45 kPa: slab and deck
*      3.7 kPa, piping, cable trays, ducts and ceiling 1.5 kPa, 25 % of a 5 kPa live load 1.25 kPa
*      (SRP 3.7.2: 25 % of the floor live load in the seismic mass)
*   3: roof slab: 37 kN/m3 x 0.15 m = 5.55 kPa: slab and deck 3.7, roofing and services 1.1, 75 % of a
*      1 kPa snow load 0.75 kPa
*   4: steel, welded: columns (welded splices and base plates) and moment-frame girders (welded flange
*      connections): E 200 GPa, nu 0.3, 7.85 t/m3 x 9.81 = 77.01 kN/m3, 2 %
*   5: steel, bolted (bearing-type bolts): braces (gusset plates), girders and beams with shear-tab
*      connections, 4 %
M,1,3.0E7,0.2,24.0,0.04,0.04,1
M,2,2.5E7,0.2,43.0,0.04,0.04,1
M,3,2.5E7,0.2,37.0,0.04,0.04,1
M,4,2.0E8,0.3,77.01,0.02,0.02,1
M,5,2.0E8,0.3,77.01,0.04,0.04,1
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------- beam sections
* R,<nm>,<A>,<As2>,<As3>,<J>,<I2>,<I3>   (m2, m4; AISC Shapes Database v15.0, 1 in = 0.0254 m)
* Local axes (spec 08 4.5): axis 1 from I to J, axis 2 toward the K node, axis 3 = 1 x 2.  I3 is the
* inertia about axis 3 and goes with bending in the 1-2 plane (deflection along axis 2, shear area
* As2).  So the K node is placed on the side of the web (the depth direction) of each W shape:
*   I3 = Ix (strong axis), I2 = Iy (weak axis), As2 = d tw (web), As3 = 5/6 x 2 bf tf (flanges).
* A tip load on a 5 m W14x132 cantilever with K in +X deflects 33.8 mm in X (P L3/(3 E Ix) + P L/(G d tw))
* and 91.7 mm in Y (weak axis): the orientation check of examples/README.md, Example 9.
*   1: W14x132  A 38.8 in2, d 14.7, bf 14.7, tw 0.645, tf 1.03 in, Ix 1530, Iy 548, J 12.3 in4
*   2: W14x90   A 26.5 in2, d 14.0, bf 14.5, tw 0.440, tf 0.710 in, Ix 999, Iy 362, J 4.06 in4
*   3: W24x76   A 22.4 in2, d 23.9, bf 8.99, tw 0.440, tf 0.680 in, Ix 2100, Iy 82.5, J 2.68 in4
*   4: W21x44   A 13.0 in2, d 20.7, bf 6.50, tw 0.350, tf 0.450 in, Ix 843, Iy 20.7, J 0.770 in4
R,1,2.50322E-2,6.11708E-3,1.62806E-2,5.11965E-6,2.28095E-4,6.36834E-4
R,2,1.70967E-2,3.97419E-3,1.10699E-2,1.68990E-6,1.50676E-4,4.15815E-4
R,3,1.44516E-2,6.78450E-3,6.57332E-3,1.11550E-6,3.43391E-5,8.74086E-4
R,4,8.38708E-3,4.67418E-3,3.14515E-3,3.20498E-7,8.61599E-6,3.50883E-4
* Braces: concentric X-braces with gusset-plate connections are pin-ended axial members.  KI/KJ cannot
* release the same moment at both ends of one element (CHECK Error 10), so the braces get I2 = I3 = 0
* (and no shear area): no bending stiffness, only the axial stiffness E A / L, as a truss member
* (ANSYS LINK180).  The real inertias are 125 in4 (HSS8x8x1/2) and 48.3 in4 (HSS6x6x1/2).  The two
* diagonals of a bay cross without a common node: a crossing node held by axial members only would
* have no stiffness out of the frame plane, and it adds no lateral stiffness to an elastic model.
*   5: HSS8x8x1/2  A 13.5 in2 (design wall 0.465 in), J 204 in4
*   6: HSS6x6x1/2  A 9.74 in2, J 81.1 in4
R,5,8.70966E-3,0,0,8.49112E-5,0,0
R,6,6.28386E-3,0,0,3.37564E-5,0,0
